## Summary

SQL injection remains one of the most critical web vulnerabilities. The fix is straightforward: always use parameterized queries and validate input. Combined with rate limiting, monitoring, and least-privilege database access, you can defend against even sophisticated injection attempts.

Key takeaways:
1. Never concatenate user input into SQL queries
2. Use parameterized queries with placeholders
3. Validate input format and length on server side
4. Monitor for injection patterns in logs
5. Apply defense in depth with rate limiting and WAF rules
6. Test security controls with automated tests
7. Prepare incident response procedures before attacks occur

The vulnerable code in this example would take an attacker minutes to exploit. The secure implementation makes successful injection effectively impossible while adding minimal performance overhead. There's no excuse for SQL injection vulnerabilities in modern applications.
